/**
\page fileM File Menu

\anchor idx_File_Menu_189

![](mfile.png)

The <b>File Menu</b> shows file oriented commands for loading and saving layout files.
<i>File Types</i> \anchor idx_File_Types_190  The main two filetypes that XTrackCAD uses are normal Layout Files (\*.xtc) and Archive Files (\*.xtce). The internals of both formats are described in the XtrkCAD Wiki at [Wiki FileFormats](http://www.xtrkcad.org/Wikka/FileFormats).

<i>Layout File</i> \anchor idx_Layout_File_191  The layout file is named with a <i>\*.xtc</i> extenstion and is a text-based description file of all the objects used in the layout plus some information about the options currently in use.
<i>Archive</i> \anchor idx_Archive_192  An archive is named with a <i>\*.xtce</i> extension, is a zipped collection of -

- a <i>manifest</i> JSON file that lists the contents,

- the .xtc layout file, and

- any other content listed in the manifest -&gt; such as referenced image files. Initially the only such content is the background image which is added to the archive if it is present.

- <b>Exit</b> - Exits <i>XTrackCAD</i>. You will be asked to confirm your choice if there are unsaved changes.

- ![](bexport.png) <b>Export</b> - Exports objects to a file in <i>XTrackCAD</i> (\*.xti) format. If no objects are selected, all objects in visible layers are exported. If objects are selected they are exported. The exported file can then be imported into another layout design. Refer to the Import command listed below.

- ![](bexportbmap.png)<b>Export to Bitmap</b> - Creates a bitmap file (\subpage cmdOutputbitmap) of the layout. The bitmap can be saved in either JPEG or PNG format.

- ![](bexportdxf.png)<b>Export to DXF</b> - Exports objects to a file in DXF format (\ref generalTerms): the current selection (\ref cmdSelect), or the scope set on the <b>Filter Layers/Groups for Print/Export</b> (\ref cmdPrintExportFilter) dialog if one is active. The exported file can then be imported into other CAD programs.

- ![](bexportsvg.png) <b>Export to SVG</b> - Exports objects to a file in SVG format: the current selection (\ref cmdSelect) if any, otherwise every visible object -- or the scope set on the <b>Filter Layers/Groups for Print/Export</b> (\ref cmdPrintExportFilter) dialog if one is active. The exported file can then be imported into a vector drawing program like Inkscape
or used for publishing on the web.

- <b>Filter Layers/Groups for Print/Export</b> - opens the \subpage cmdPrintExportFilter dialog to scope <b>Print</b> (\ref cmdPrint) and <b>Export to DXF</b>/<b>SVG</b> to specific <b>Layers</b> or <b>Layer Groups</b> (\ref cmdLayergroup).

- ![](bimport.png) <b>Import</b> - Displays the file selection dialog which allows selection of an existing <i>XTrackCAD</i> Import (\subpage cmdExport \*.xti) files. This function can be used to transfer parts like a yard design from one layout design to another.

- ![](bimportmod.png) <b>Import Module</b> - Displays the file selection dialog which allows selection of an existing <i>XTrackCAD</i> Import (\ref cmdExport \*.xti) file. The imported parts are put into a empty layer, sets the layer name to the imported filename and sets the layer to be a module - see (\ref cmdLayer).

- ![](bimportdxf.png) <b>Import DXF</b> - Displays a dialog which allows selection of options for (\subpage cmdImportDxf \*.dxf) file. The "Layer 0 Track" option will create track from lines (STRAIGHT) and arcs (CURVE) in DXF layer 0. The DXF file is converted to an XTI file in the same folder as the DXF file. If "Import XTI" is slected, the XTI will be imported into the layout. if "Import Module" is selected it will be imported into an empty layer as a module - see (\ref cmdLayer).

- <b>File List</b> - List of the five most recently used <i>XTrackCAD</i> files or archives.

- ![](bnew.png) <b>New</b> - Clears the current layout.  In case there are any unsaved changes on the current plan, a warning pop-up will be displayed and you'll have the option to cancel the operation. After that the Layout Options (\ref cmdLayout) dialog will be opened.

- <b>Notes</b> - Displays a simple notepad editor which you can use to enter information about the layout. Typically this could include prototype, period, theme, layout style, height, construction, type of track and turnouts used, control system and anything else of interest.  (There is also a <b>Note</b> command (\ref cmdNote) which allows attaching notes to particular parts of the layout.)

- ![](bopen.png) <b>Open</b> - Displays the <b>File Open</b> dialog which allows an existing <i>XTrackCAD</i> file or archive to be selected and opened.  In case there are any unsaved changes on the current plan, a warning pop-up will be displayed and you'll have the option to cancel the operation.

- ![](bparam.png) <b>Parameter Files</b> - Displays the <b>Parameter Files</b> dialog (\subpage cmdPrmfile) to choose parameter files.

- ![](bsetup.png) <b>Print Setup</b> - Shows the Printer Setup Dialog (\subpage printSetup).

- ![](bprint.png) <b>Print</b> - Invokes the Print command (\subpage cmdPrint).

- ![](brevert.png) <b>Revert</b> - Undoes all changes and returns to the last saved layout plan.

- ![](bsave.png) <b>Save</b> - Saves the layout under the current file name and with the same file type as was opened. If a filename has not yet been assigned then the <b>File Save</b> dialog is shown so you enter a file (.xtc) or archive name (.xtce).

- ![](bsaveas.png) <b>Save As</b> - This command lets you make a copy of the track plan you are currently working on as an file (.xtc) or an archive (.xtce). It differs from the regular Save command. Save stores your data back into the folder (directory) it originally came from in the same filetype. "Save As" lets you give your plan a different name and/or put it in a different folder on your hard disk and chnage its filetype using the selection box at the bottom of the list of files or by hardcoding the extensions .xtc or .xtce (for an archive).

- \subpage ExportDXF
- \subpage ExportSVG
- \subpage cmdImport
- \subpage cmdImportM
- \subpage cmdSearchgui
- \subpage cmdPrintMargin

*/

/**
\page cmdExport Export

\anchor idx_Export_193

![](bexport.png)

This menu item displays a file selection dialog where a file name for exported objects can be entered.  All selected (\ref cmdSelect) objects are exported to the file. The export file will have an `xti` extension.

Using the Import command (\ref cmdImport), an exported file can be imported into another layout design.

*/

/**
\page cmdOutputbitmap Export to Bitmap

\anchor idx_Export_to_Bitmap_194

![](bexportbmap.png)

This menu item creates a bitmap file of the layout.

![](bitmap.png)

The <b>Bitmap</b> dialog specifies the style and size of the bitmap.  The check-boxes (<b>Layout Titles</b>, <b>Borders</b>, <b>Centerline of Track</b> and
<b>Background Image</b>) control
whether the Layout Title (\ref cmdLayout), the borders or the track centerlines are printed on the bitmap. If a background image is used it will be
printed if the option is set.

 The size of the bitmap is smaller if these are disabled.

Printing the track centerlines (also seen when zoomed in 1:1) is useful when you later print the bitmap
full size for laying out track.

If the Snap Grid (\ref cmdGrid) is visible then it will be printed on the bitmap.

The <b>DPI</b> control specifies the number of pixels per inch in the bitmap.  Bitmaps must be less than 32,000 pixels in height or width.
The upper value you can enter depends on the size of your trackplan. It is made sure that your bitmap does not exceed these limits.
Larger values will result in a larger bitmap file.

The bitmap width, height and approximate file size is indicated.

Note: This command can create a very large file and consume a lot of memory and time.

Pressing the <b>OK</b> button invokes a <b>File Save</b> dialog so you can choose the file name for the Bitmap.

You can select to create JPEG or PNG files. As a rule of thumb JPEG results in smaller files  when you use a backgound image, PNG
does so for trackplans without an image background.

*/

/**
\page ExportDXF Export to DXF

\anchor idx_Export_to_DXF_195

![](bexportdxf.png)

This menu item displays a file selection dialog where a file name for exported objects can be entered. If the <b>Filter Layers/Groups for Print/Export</b> dialog (\ref cmdPrintExportFilter) has an active scope, every visible object on an included Layer is exported regardless of selection; otherwise, the current selection (\ref cmdSelect) is exported -- and at least one of the two is required, or the command reports that nothing is selected. The export file will have an `dxf` extension.

Exported files can then be imported into other CAD programs.

*/

/**
\page ExportSVG Export to SVG

\anchor idx_Export_to_SVG_196

![](bexportsvg.png)

This menu item displays a file selection dialog where a file name for exported objects can be entered. If the <b>Filter Layers/Groups for Print/Export</b> dialog (\ref cmdPrintExportFilter) has an active scope, every visible object on an included Layer is exported regardless of selection; otherwise the current selection (\ref cmdSelect) is exported if there is one, or every visible object if nothing is selected. The export file will have an `svg` extension.

Files in SVG format can be used for different purposes.

- SVG files can be loaded into many drawing programs. This makes it possible to enhance and resize the image. Vector images have the advantage of
allowing lossless resizing.

- Most modern browsers support SVG as an image format. So the resulting file can directly be used to publish your plan on the web. By adding a bit of
Javascript, zooming and panning can be enabled. Appropiate scripts can easily be found using your favorite search engine.
The generated SVG uses the layout description as a title that is shown by some browser. Also the drawing has an 'id' for manipulation by Javascript.
This id is derived from the filename by removing all spaces and sticking the word 'id' in front. Eg exporting `stonega.xtc` the resulting id will be `idstonega`.

*/

/**
\page cmdPrintExportFilter Filter Layers/Groups for Print/Export

![](dprintexportfilter.png)

The <b>Filter Layers/Groups for Print/Export</b> dialog scopes <b>Print</b>
(\ref cmdPrint), <b>Export to DXF</b> (\ref ExportDXF), and <b>Export to
SVG</b> (\ref ExportSVG) down to specific <b>Layers</b> and/or <b>Layer
Groups</b> (\ref cmdLayergroup) -- the same Available/Included/Groups
dialog and mechanics as the Reports menu's own <b>Filter Layers/Groups</b>
dialog (\ref cmdReportsfilter), but a single filter shared across Print
and both Export commands, not one per report.

Unlike a report's filter, this one only ever narrows what a <b>Layer</b>'s
own visibility (\ref cmdLayer) already allows through -- including a
hidden layer here does not make it print or export; it still has to be
made visible first. Once at least one layer is included, it also fully
replaces the current selection (\ref cmdSelect) as the export scope for
DXF/SVG, rather than requiring you to select matching tracks by hand
first.

Reachable from the <b>File</b> menu (\ref fileM), or from the <b>Filter
Layers/Groups...</b> button on the <b>Print</b> (\ref cmdPrint) dialog --
both open and edit the same shared filter.

The <b>Clear Filter</b> button restores the unfiltered state: <b>Print</b>
scoped only by <b>Layer</b> visibility, and <b>Export</b> scoped only by
the current selection (or every visible object, for SVG, if nothing is
selected).

The <b>Done</b> button closes the dialog.

*/

/**
\page cmdImport Import

\anchor idx_Import_197

![](bimport.png)

Importing displays a file selection dialog to specify the file to import.

After importing the objects from the file, the imported objects are in selected state and therefore are drawn in red. This allows you to easily move and rotate the imported objects to their final position.

*/

/**
\page cmdImportM Import as Module

\anchor idx_Import_as_Module_198

![](bimportmod.png)

Importing displays a file selection dialog to specify the file to import as a module.

After importing the objects from the file, the imported objects are in selected state and therefore are drawn in red. This allows you to easily move and rotate the imported objects to their final position.

*/

/**
\page cmdImportDxf Import Dxf File

\anchor idx_Import_Dxf_File_199

![](bimportdxf.png)

Importing displays a file selection dialog to specify the DXF file to import.

This menu item displays option for importing a DXF file. The command actually creates a XTI file that mirrors the contents of the DXF. It is in the same folderr and has the same filename as the DXF. The "Layer 0 Track" option outputs Lines and Arcs in DXF layer 0 as Straight and Curved tracks. The "Import XTI" option immediately imports the XTI file into the layout. "Import as Module" imports the XTI as a module. The Ok button opens a file selection dialog where a DXF file is selected. After importing the objects from the file, the imported objects are in selected state and therefore are drawn in red. This allows you to easily move and rotate the imported objects to their final position.

*/

/**
\page cmdPrmfile Parameter Files

\anchor idx_Parameter_Files_200

![](bparam.png)

<i>Parameter Files</i> contain turnout, sectional track and structure definitions.  Loaded Parameter Files are managed with the <b>Parameter Files</b> dialog.

![](dprmfile.png)

The dialog contains a list of parameter files. Each file is preceded by a color indicator. These indicators show whether a parameter file contains definitions that
are usable in the current scale and gauge.

![](greendot.png) ![](greenstar.png) Definitions from this file are a perfect fit for the current layout options. The file has been loaded and will be visible in the <b>HotBar</b>. For track this means that gauge and scale of the track elements
are identical to the layout settings. Prototype definitions (Scale "\*") are always a perfect fit. Turnouts and Sectional Track must have the same gauge and usually the same scale unless the scale/gauge combination has been defined to fit.
OO layouts use HO track as an example.

![](yellowdot.png) ![](yellowstar.png) Definitions from this file can be used with this layout but do not fit perfectly. A typical example is On30 where HO scale track can be used because the
gauge is the same. But differences in scale may make the track look out of scale. Structures are considered compatible if the scale is within +/-20%. Cars are compatible if the gauge is the same, but the scale can be +/-20%.
Exceptions to this can be defined in the scale definitions.

![](reddot.png) ![](redstar.png) This parameter file doesn't contain any usable definitions with current settings. You may want to change the layout options if you want to use elements from
this file (for example altering the Layout Scale).

![](greydot.png) ![](greystar.png) This file is loaded but hidden.
If it is marked as favorite it will be kept on the
list between runs.

Parameter files can be marked as <i>favorite</i>. The indicator for favorites is a star.
The meaning of the colors isn't changed.

Loaded definition files that are marked with a yellow or a green indicator appear on the <b>Hot Bar</b> (\ref cmdHotBar).

If you <i>unload</i> a file the definitions are removed from the selection lists and the <b>Hot Bar</b>.
If the unloaded file was marked as a favorite it can be <i>reloaded</i> by selecting the <b>Reload</b> button.

Files that are still loaded can be <i>reloaded</i> as well. In that case the old definitions are refreshed and extended by the
rereading the file. This can be useful if you want to check changes you made by hand-editing a parameter file.

Note that loading a file does not change the existing definitions in the layout. To do that after loading, use \ref cmdRefreshSpecial.

It is possible to change the state of several files at once. The <b>Select All</b> button allows you to select all parameter files for the next operation.

In case unloaded files are marked as favorite, their state will be preserved
between runs of the program.
This makes it possible to keep a list of preferred parameter sets between
runs of XTrackCAD but hide temporary unused files from the hotbar. That
way the hotbar contents can be kept short containing only the currently needed elements.

In order to update the defintion of an already loaded file from disk, you can use the <i>Reload</i> button. This will reload the file contents - with any changes made.
It is the equivalent of using <b>Browse</b> and selecting the file again.

Files can be listed by either their file name or by their contents description.
This is controlled by the <b>Show File Names</b> check box.

The <b>Library</b> button opens the <i>Choose Parameter File</i> dialog (\ref cmdSearchgui) this
shows the all files included the system library so they can be added.

The <b>Browse</b> button opens a file window that allows the user to add additional files
to directly load other non-system <i>parameter files</i> such as provided by other users or
downloaded from the Internet.

When first installed, parameter files for all common scales are loaded.
You can unload parameter files you don't use to speed up <i>XTrackCAD</i> startup time.

The order in this list controls the order of turnouts and structures on the
<b>Hot Bar</b> (\ref cmdHotBar). You can move a parameter file to the end of the
list by <i>Unloading</i> it, and then loading it via the <b>Browse</b> button.

*/

/**
\page cmdSearchgui Choose Parameter Files

\anchor idx_Choose_Parameter_Files_201

Selecting and loading additional parameter files from the System Library is done in the
<b>Choose Parameter Files</b> dialog.

![](paramsearch.png)

Using the Search function you can find and select parameter files that are
part of the XTrackCAD installation.
Enter a set of one or more words and select the Search button. Search results are
shown in the list box. Pressing the <i>Clear Filter</i> \anchor idx_Clear_Filter_202  button shows the complete
list again.

You can subset the search to only search for parameter files who start with contents that fit the current Layout Scale using the <b>Fit</b> Mode.
The options are "Fit All", "Fit Compatible" and "Fit Exact".  Fit Compatible means that structures with
Note that the code only looks at the first Turnout, Structure or Car entry to decide on the scale for the entire file.
They will correspond to the color icons on the Parameter Files menu once they are loaded \ref cmdPrmfile when the same Layout scale is in use.

Select the files you're interested in and press the <b>Add</b> button to add them to your
parameter list.

*/

/**
\page cmdPrint Printing

\anchor idx_Printing_203

![](bprint.png)

The <b>Print</b> command (<b>File&gt;Print</b>, <i>Ctrl+P</i>) prints all or part of the layout, at a scale you choose.

Printing has two steps:

1. In the XTrackCAD <b>Print</b> dialog you choose the print scale, which pages to print and what to put on them.  While the dialog is open, a grid on the layout shows how the layout is divided into pages.

2. When you press <b>Print</b>, the system's print dialog opens.  There you choose the printer (or print to a PDF file) and the number of copies.

For step-by-step examples, such as a one-page overview or a print at a chosen scale, see \subpage printHowTo.

<h3>The print grid</h3>

![](printgrid.png)

Each rectangle of the grid is one printed page.  Selected pages are shaded.

- Click a page to select it, click it again to deselect it.

- `Shift+Left+Drag` moves the grid, `Shift+Right+Drag` rotates it.  The <b>Advanced</b> tab shows the resulting origin and angle.

<h3>Page Setup tab</h3>

![](print.png)

- <b>Print Scale</b> - the reduction from layout to paper, as 1:<i>Print Scale</i>.  At 24, 24 inches of layout print as 1 inch on paper.  The value is a whole number from 1 (full size) to 999.  Text on the layout shrinks with the print scale, the same way it does when you zoom out on screen.

- <b>Page Width</b>, <b>Page Height</b> - how much of the layout one page covers, in layout units.  For example, at a Print Scale of 24, a <b>Page Width</b> of 192.000 means each page covers 16 feet of layout.  You can enter smaller values, but not more than the page holds at this scale.

- <b>Max</b> - sets <b>Page Width</b> and <b>Page Height</b> to the most that fits on the paper at this scale.

- <b>Snap Shot</b> - picks the <b>Print Scale</b> and <b>Page Format</b> that fit the whole layout on one page, and selects that page.  It fits the room size set in the Layout dialog (\ref cmdLayout), not just the objects you have drawn.

- <b>Page Format</b> - <i>Portrait</i> or <i>Landscape</i>.  XTrackCAD turns the page itself, so use this setting rather than the orientation in the system dialogs.

- <b>Print Order</b> - relative to the print grid, <i>Normal</i> prints columns first, top to bottom, left to right.  <i>Reverse</i> prints rows first, left to right, bottom to top.

<h3>Content tab</h3>

![](printcontent.png)

- <b>Engineering Data</b> - adds a box at the bottom of each page with the layout title and subtitle (\ref cmdLayout), the date, the print scale, the room size, the model scale and the file name.  The box takes 1 inch of the page.

- <b>Page Numbers</b> - prints the page number in the center of each page as (x/y).  x is the page position from left to right, y from bottom to top, so (1/1) is the page at the bottom left.  The numbers of the neighboring pages are printed on each edge.

- <b>Registration Marks</b> - prints alignment crosses every 3" (or 10cm).  Only available at a <b>Print Scale</b> of 1.  This helps to line up pages printed at different angles.

- <b>Snap Grid</b> - prints the Snap Grid (\ref cmdGrid).  Only available while the Snap Grid is shown.

- <b>Centerline Below Scale 1:1</b> - prints a dotted line down the center of each track.  The centerline is always printed at 1:1; this adds it at smaller scales.  Disabled when tracks print with a single line.

- <b>Roadbed Outline</b> - prints lines along the edges of the roadbed, if <b>Width</b> is greater than the track gauge.  Most useful at 1:1, for cutting sub-roadbed.  Disabled when tracks print with a single line.

- <b>Print Rulers</b> - prints rulers along the edge of the layout (<i>Layout Edge</i>), along the edges of every page (<i>Every Page</i>), or not at all (<i>None</i>).  The rulers show layout distances, so they are a quick check of the printed scale.  Only available when the grid <b>Angle</b> is 0.

Whether tracks print with one line or two (center line and rails) depends on the <b>Print Scale</b>.  If the Print Scale is no more than twice the <b>Two Rail Scale</b> on the Display dialog (\ref cmdDisplay), tracks print with two lines.  At 1:1 the center line is printed too.

<h3>Advanced tab</h3>

![](printadvanced.png)

- <b>Origin: X</b>, <b>Y</b> and <b>Angle</b> - the position and rotation of the print grid on the layout.  Type values here, or drag the grid with the mouse.  <b>Reset</b> sets them to 0.

- <b>Page Margins: Ignore</b> - uses the whole sheet instead of the printable area, so pages can be placed edge to edge without trimming.  Most printers can't print right to the edge, so a thin strip around each page may stay blank.

- <b>Margins</b> - shows and changes the margins for the current printer (\ref cmdPrintMargin).

<h3>Buttons</h3>

- <b>Filter Layers/Groups...</b> - prints only some Layers or Layer Groups (\ref cmdPrintExportFilter), without changing which layers are visible.  Hidden layers never print.

- <b>Setup</b> - opens Printer Setup (\ref printSetup) to choose the paper size.

- <b>Clear</b> - deselects all pages.

- <b>Select All</b> - selects every page of the grid that covers the layout.

- <b>Print</b> - opens the system print dialog for the selected pages.  It is only enabled once at least one page is selected.  After printing, the <b>Print</b> dialog closes and the page selection is cleared; the other settings are kept.

*/

/**
\page printHowTo Printing How-To

Each example starts from <b>File&gt;Print</b> (<i>Ctrl+P</i>).  The settings are explained on the Printing page (\ref cmdPrint).

Try a new kind of printout with a PDF file first (see <i>Check a printout on screen first</i> below).  It costs no paper and shows exactly what the printer will get.

<h3>One-page overview of the whole layout</h3>

1. Check the room size in the Layout dialog (\ref cmdLayout): <b>Snap Shot</b> fits the room, not just the objects you have drawn.  If the drawing only fills part of the room, the overview will be smaller than it needs to be.

2. Optional: on the <b>Content</b> tab, check <b>Engineering Data</b> to add the layout title, scale and date.  Uncheck <b>Page Numbers</b>, since there is only one page.

3. On the <b>Page Setup</b> tab, press <b>Snap Shot</b>.  XTrackCAD chooses the <b>Print Scale</b> and <b>Page Format</b> that fit everything on one page, and selects that page (<i>1 page selected</i>).

4. Press <b>Print</b>, choose the printer or PDF in the system dialog and print.

<b>One page per level.</b>  If each level of the layout is on its own Layer or Layer Group, you can print a matching overview for each one:

1. Set up the overview as above and press <b>Snap Shot</b> once.  The scale stays the same for every level, so the pages can be compared or overlaid.

2. Press <b>Filter Layers/Groups...</b> and include only the layers (or the Layer Group) of the first level.  Close the filter dialog with <b>Done</b>.  The layers must also be visible (\ref cmdLayer); the filter never prints a hidden layer.

3. Press <b>Print</b> and print the page.

4. Open <b>Print</b> again and press <b>Snap Shot</b> again: printing clears the page selection, and <b>Snap Shot</b> picks the same scale every time.  Change the filter to the next level and print again.  Repeat for each level.

5. Afterwards, press <b>Clear Filter</b> in the filter dialog so later prints and exports include everything again.

Tip: the Engineering Data box shows the layout title and subtitle.  To label each page with its level, change the subtitle in the Layout dialog (\ref cmdLayout) before printing that level.

<h3>Print at a chosen scale</h3>

For example, to make 1 foot of layout 1/2 inch on paper:

1. Work out the Print Scale: divide the layout distance by the paper distance, in the same units.  1 foot = 12 inches, and 12 / 0.5 = 24, so the Print Scale is <b>24</b> (1:24).

2. On the <b>Page Setup</b> tab, enter 24 in <b>Print Scale</b> and press <i>Tab</i>.  The grid on the layout is redrawn for the new page size, and <b>Page Width</b> and <b>Page Height</b> show how much layout each page covers.

3. Choose the <b>Page Format</b> (Portrait or Landscape) that covers the layout with fewer pages.

4. Select the pages to print: click them on the layout, or press <b>Select All</b>.  To move the grid so the layout falls on fewer pages, use `Shift+Left+Drag`, or set the origin on the <b>Advanced</b> tab.

5. On the <b>Content</b> tab, set <b>Print Rulers</b> to <i>Layout Edge</i> or <i>Every Page</i>, so you can check the scale on the printout.

6. Press <b>Print</b>.  In the system print dialog, make sure the printer does not resize the page: scale 100%, one page per sheet, and no <i>Fit to page</i>.

7. Check the printout: the 1-foot marks on the ruler should be 1/2 inch apart.

Some common choices:

| Layout distance | Paper distance | Print Scale |
|-----------------|----------------|-------------|
| 1 foot          | 1 inch         | 12          |
| 1 foot          | 1/2 inch       | 24          |
| 1 foot          | 1/4 inch       | 48          |
| 1 foot          | 1/8 inch       | 96          |
| 1 meter         | 2 cm           | 50          |
| 1 meter         | 1 cm           | 100         |
| full size       | full size      | 1           |

The layout distance is the size of the layout as drawn: the real benchwork, not the prototype it models.  The Print Scale has to be a whole number.

<h3>Full-size (1:1) templates</h3>

Printing at a Print Scale of 1 gives full-size templates to lay track or cut sub-roadbed from.

1. Enter 1 in <b>Print Scale</b>.

2. On the <b>Content</b> tab, check <b>Registration Marks</b>, so the pages can be lined up.  For cutting sub-roadbed, also check <b>Roadbed Outline</b> and set its <b>Width</b>.

3. On the <b>Advanced</b> tab, check <b>Page Margins: Ignore</b>, so the pages can be taped together edge to edge.

4. Select only the pages you need and print them.

If you print strips at different angles to follow the track around a curve, the registration marks let you line the strips up.  To transfer the plan without printing at 1:1, show the Snap Grid (\ref cmdGrid) lined up with your room and print it with <b>Snap Grid</b> on the <b>Content</b> tab.  You may want to change the Snap Grid color (\ref cmdRgbcolor) so it stands out on paper.

<h3>Check a printout on screen first</h3>

To see the result before using paper, print to a PDF file and open it in a PDF viewer:

- On Linux, choose <b>Print to File</b> in the system print dialog and select <i>PDF</i>.

- On Windows, choose the <b>Microsoft Print to PDF</b> printer.

- On macOS, choose <b>Print to File</b> as on Linux.

<h3>Troubleshooting</h3>

- <b>The Print button is disabled.</b> No pages are selected.  Click pages on the layout, or press <b>Select All</b> or <b>Snap Shot</b>.

- <b>More pages than expected.</b> The number of pages depends on the <b>Print Scale</b>: the smaller the number, the larger the printout.  Use <b>Snap Shot</b> for a single page, or raise the Print Scale.

- <b>The printout is not to scale.</b> Check that the system print dialog and the printer driver print at 100%, with no <i>Fit to page</i> or <i>Shrink to fit</i>.  Then check the printer margins (\ref cmdPrintMargin).

- <b>Something is missing from the printout.</b> Only visible layers print (\ref cmdLayer).  If the <b>Filter Layers/Groups</b> dialog (\ref cmdPrintExportFilter) has layers included, only those layers print.

- <b>The overview is smaller than it could be.</b> <b>Snap Shot</b> fits the room size in the Layout dialog (\ref cmdLayout).  Make the room size fit the drawing, or set the Print Scale by hand and move the grid.

- <b>Text is the wrong size.</b> Text on the layout prints at the size it has at the chosen <b>Print Scale</b>, just as it looks when zoomed out on screen.  Print to PDF to check, and report the problem with the PDF and your layout file if the text does not match the screen.

*/

/**
\page printSetup Printer Setup

\anchor idx_Printer_Setup_204

The <b>Printer Setup</b> command (<b>File&gt;Print Setup</b>), and the <b>Setup</b> button on the <b>Print</b> dialog (\ref cmdPrint), open the system's page setup dialog.  It looks different on each system:

- \subpage Linux
- \subpage MS-Windows

In either dialog, choose the printer and the paper size.  Ignore the orientation: use the <b>Page Format</b> on the <b>Print</b> dialog instead.  XTrackCAD remembers the settings for the next print.

XTrackCAD starts from the margins the system reports for the printer and paper.  If your printer's printable area is different, adjust it with <b>Margins</b> on the <b>Print</b> dialog's <b>Advanced</b> tab (\ref cmdPrintMargin).  Those values are saved for each printer.

<h3>Advanced: print scale overrides</h3>

Two settings in the <i>Preference</i> section of the XTrackCAD configuration file can override how drawings and text are scaled for printing.  They are only needed to work around a printer driver that reports its resolution wrongly, and are not set by default:

- <i>Preference.PrintScale</i> - if greater than 0, drawing coordinates are scaled by this factor relative to 72 dpi.

- <i>Preference.PrintTextScale</i> - if greater than 0, text is scaled by this factor.  Only used when <i>PrintScale</i> is set.

If they are not set in the configuration file, XTrackCAD uses the environment variables <i>XTRKCADPRINTSCALE</i> and <i>XTRKCADPRINTTEXTSCALE</i> instead.

*/

/**
\page Linux Linux

\anchor idx_Linux_205

![](printset.png)

The Linux <b>Page Setup</b> dialog contains:

- <b>Format for</b> - the printer to set up.  Choose <i>Any Printer</i> for a general paper size.

- <b>Paper size</b> - the paper loaded in the printer.

- <b>Orientation</b> - ignored by XTrackCAD; use the <b>Page Format</b> on the <b>Print</b> dialog.

- <b>Apply</b> saves the settings, <b>Cancel</b> discards them.

*/

/**
\page MS-Windows Microsoft Windows

\anchor idx_Microsoft_Windows_206

On Windows, <b>Printer Setup</b> opens the standard Windows <b>Page Setup</b> dialog.  It contains:

- <b>Paper Size</b> and <b>Source</b> - the paper and the tray to use.

- <b>Orientation</b> - ignored by XTrackCAD; use the <b>Page Format</b> on the <b>Print</b> dialog.

- <b>Margins</b> - the margins XTrackCAD starts from.  Fine-tune them with the <b>Margins</b> button on the <b>Print</b> dialog (\ref cmdPrintMargin).

- <b>Printer...</b> - chooses the printer.

*/

/**
\page cmdPrintMargin Printer Margin

\anchor idx_Printer_Margin_207

![](printmargin.png)

The <b>Printer Margins</b> dialog allows you to configure the selected printer's margins.

The margins for the four edges of the printed page are displayed.
The default values are fetched from the Operating System.
You can enter updated values if the System values don't match your actual printer.

The <b>Reset</b> is enabled if the displayed values don't match the System values and will reload the System's values.

To check your printer's margin:

- Set <b>Scale</b> to 1

- Set <b>Origin</b> and <b>Angle</b> to 0.000 by clicking the <b>Reset</b> button

- Enable <b>Registration Marks</b> this prints crosshairs every 3" or 10cm at scale 1:1.

- Enable <b>Ignore Page Margins</b>

- Select and <b>Print</b> the lower left page of you layout

- Measure the distance from the left and bottom edges to the Registration Mark at (3",3") or (10cm,10cm).

- Update the <b>Printer Margin</b> values for the left and bottom edges.  You can also update the top and right edges.

Margins values are stored for each printer.

You can also load <b>PrinterMarginTest-A4.xtc</b> or <b>PrinterMarginTest-Letter.xtc</b> from <b>Help|Examples</b>.  These files have registration lines in the corners to help determine your printer's margins.

*/

